    "lem-energy-based-density-is-a-probability-measure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-energy-based-density-and-partition-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $\\Theta$ be a parameter set,\nand for each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ let $E_\\theta:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be a measurable\nenergy function. Its **partition function relative to $\\mu$** is\n$$Z_\\theta:=\\int_X e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x).$$\nThe admissible parameter set is\n$$\\Theta_\\mu:=\\{\\theta\\in\\Theta:0<Z_\\theta<\\infty\\}.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\Theta_\\mu$, the **energy-based probability measure** is the\nmeasure with density $e^{-E_\\theta}/Z_\\theta$ relative to $\\mu$:\n$$P_\\theta(A):=\\frac{1}{Z_\\theta}\\int_A e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x),\\qquad A\\in\\mathcal A.$$\nThe restriction $0<Z_\\theta<\\infty$ is part of the definition; parameters\noutside $\\Theta_\\mu$ do not define a normalized model by this formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-measure-with-density",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable. The **measure with density $f$ relative\nto $\\mu$** is the measure\n$$f\\,d\\mu:\\mathcal A\\to[0,+\\infty],\\qquad (f\\,d\\mu)(A):=\\int_A f\\,d\\mu,$$\nwhose measure property is supplied by\n[[thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, the series $\\sum_{n\\ge0}x^n/\\iota(n!)$ converges absolutely. Its power-series radius is therefore $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-power-series-sums-are-continuous",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f(x)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}a_n(x-c)^n$ for $|x-c|<R$, then $f$ is continuous at every $x_0$ satisfying $|x_0-c|<R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:X\\to Y$ is continuous between topological spaces, then\n$f^{-1}[B]\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B(Y)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-composition-with-borel-functions-preserves-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal{A})$, $(Y,\\mathcal{B})$, and $(Z,\\mathcal{C})$ be measurable\nspaces. If $f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Y,\\mathcal{B})$ is measurable and\n$g : (Y,\\mathcal{B}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable, then\n$g \\circ f : (X,\\mathcal{A}) \\to (Z,\\mathcal{C})$ is measurable.\n\nIn particular, if $f$ is measurable and $g$ is a Borel measurable function on\nits codomain, then $g \\circ f$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\exp(x)>0$ and\n$$\\exp(-x)=\\frac1{\\exp(x)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Define $\\phi(t):=\\exp(-t)$. By [F4], $\\phi(t)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}(-1)^nt^n/n!$ is a power-series sum with infinite radius, so [F5] makes $\\phi$ continuous on $\\mathbb R$. Thus [F6] makes it Borel measurable, and [F7] with the measurable energy from [F1] makes $x\\mapsto\\exp(-E_\\theta(x))$ measurable. Its values are positive by [F8], hence nonnegative. Put $M_\\theta(A):=\\int_A e^{-E_\\theta}\\,d\\mu$. Now [F2] makes $M_\\theta$ a measure and $M_\\theta(X)=Z_\\theta$. Thus $P_\\theta(\\varnothing)=Z_\\theta^{-1}M_\\theta(\\varnothing)=0$. For any pairwise disjoint sequence $(A_n)_{n\\ge0}$, [F2] and finite-sum homogeneity give $P_\\theta(\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}A_n)=Z_\\theta^{-1}\\sum_{n\\ge0}M_\\theta(A_n)=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\sum_{n=0}^N Z_\\theta^{-1}M_\\theta(A_n)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}P_\\theta(A_n)$. Therefore $P_\\theta$ is a measure.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Its total mass is $P_\\theta(X)=Z_\\theta^{-1}M_\\theta(X)=1$, so [F3] makes $P_\\theta$ a probability measure.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
