    "thm-score-matching-integration-by-parts-identity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-energy-based-density-and-partition-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $\\Theta$ be a parameter set,\nand for each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ let $E_\\theta:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be a measurable\nenergy function. Its **partition function relative to $\\mu$** is\n$$Z_\\theta:=\\int_X e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x).$$\nThe admissible parameter set is\n$$\\Theta_\\mu:=\\{\\theta\\in\\Theta:0<Z_\\theta<\\infty\\}.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\Theta_\\mu$, the **energy-based probability measure** is the\nmeasure with density $e^{-E_\\theta}/Z_\\theta$ relative to $\\mu$:\n$$P_\\theta(A):=\\frac{1}{Z_\\theta}\\int_A e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x),\\qquad A\\in\\mathcal A.$$\nThe restriction $0<Z_\\theta<\\infty$ is part of the definition; parameters\noutside $\\Theta_\\mu$ do not define a normalized model by this formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-measure-with-density",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable. The **measure with density $f$ relative\nto $\\mu$** is the measure\n$$f\\,d\\mu:\\mathcal A\\to[0,+\\infty],\\qquad (f\\,d\\mu)(A):=\\int_A f\\,d\\mu,$$\nwhose measure property is supplied by\n[[thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Then:\n\n1. $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and\n   $\\lambda_n$ is a measure on it ([[def-measure]]);\n2. the measure space $(\\mathbb{R}^n, \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n), \\lambda_n)$ is\n   complete ([[def-complete-measure-space]]), and every $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$\n   with $\\lambda_n^*(S) = 0$ is Lebesgue measurable with $\\lambda_n(S) = 0$;\n3. every elementary set is Lebesgue measurable and\n   $\\lambda_n(A) = \\mu_0(A)$ for every $A \\in \\mathcal{E}_n$; in particular\n   $\\lambda_n(B) = \\operatorname{vol}(B)$ for every half-open box $B$,\n   $\\lambda_n(\\varnothing) = 0$ and $\\lambda_n(\\mathbb{R}^n) = +\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is\n**continuously differentiable**, or of class $C^1$, when it is totally\ndifferentiable at every point of $U$ and the entries of its derivative matrix\nare continuous functions on $U$.\n\nFor an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $a\\in U$, a **local inverse of $f$ at\n$a$** is a function $g:W\\to V$ for open neighbourhoods $a\\in V\\subseteq U$\nand $f(a)\\in W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ such that $f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective and\n$g=(f|_V)^{-1}$. If both $f|_V$ and $g$ are $C^1$, this restriction is a\n**local diffeomorphism** at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, $n\\ge1$, let\n$u,v\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$, and let\n$\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N_0^n$ be the multi-index of\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]. We say that $v$ is\nthe **weak derivative** $D^\\alpha u$ if\n$$\\int_\\Omega u\\,D^\\alpha\\varphi\\,dx =(-1)^{|\\alpha|}\\int_\\Omega v\\varphi\\,dx \\qquad\\text{for every }\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega).$$\nBoth integrals are finite because the test and all its derivatives are bounded\nand compactly supported. The test identity is choice-free. By\n[[def-locally-integrable-function-as-a-regular-distribution]] and the signed\ntranspose convention in [[def-distributional-derivative]], it is equivalent\nto $\\partial^\\alpha T_u=T_v$ as distributions. For $\\alpha=0$, this identity\nreduces to $T_u=T_v$, hence to $u=v$ almost everywhere by the published\nembedding theorem under [[def-countable-choice]].\n\n**Well-definedness.** Changing either representative on a null set leaves each\ntest integral unchanged. Weak derivatives are therefore statements about\nalmost-everywhere classes; existence and uniqueness of their locally\nintegrable value classes are audited separately.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-weak-derivatives-are-unique-almost-everywhere",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, $n\\ge1$,\n$\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N_0^n$, and $u,v,w\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$. If both\n$v$ and $w$ are weak $\\alpha$-derivatives of $u$, then\n$$v=w\\quad\\text{almost everywhere on }\\Omega.$$\nThus a weak derivative, when it exists, is unique as an almost-everywhere\nclass. No connectedness assumption is needed.\n\nIf $\\Omega=\\varnothing$, the assertion holds because there is only the zero\nclass and no nonzero tests.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
