    "thm-contrastive-divergence-k-gradient-omits-chain-derivative": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-energy-based-density-and-partition-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $\\Theta$ be a parameter set,\nand for each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ let $E_\\theta:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be a measurable\nenergy function. Its **partition function relative to $\\mu$** is\n$$Z_\\theta:=\\int_X e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x).$$\nThe admissible parameter set is\n$$\\Theta_\\mu:=\\{\\theta\\in\\Theta:0<Z_\\theta<\\infty\\}.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\Theta_\\mu$, the **energy-based probability measure** is the\nmeasure with density $e^{-E_\\theta}/Z_\\theta$ relative to $\\mu$:\n$$P_\\theta(A):=\\frac{1}{Z_\\theta}\\int_A e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x),\\qquad A\\in\\mathcal A.$$\nThe restriction $0<Z_\\theta<\\infty$ is part of the definition; parameters\noutside $\\Theta_\\mu$ do not define a normalized model by this formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-contrastive-divergence-k",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $r$ be a positive\ninteger and $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^r$,\nand let $E_\\theta:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable in $x$ and continuously\ndifferentiable in $\\theta$. Define the energy model $P_\\theta$ relative to $\\mu$\nonly when\n$$0<Z_\\theta:=\\int_\\mathcal X e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x)<\\infty,$$\nwith density $e^{-E_\\theta}/Z_\\theta$. Let $Q_0$ be a data probability\nmeasure on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ and let $K_\\theta$ be a probability kernel\nfrom $\\mathcal X$ to itself which leaves $P_\\theta$ invariant, meaning\n$$\\int_\\mathcal X K_\\theta(x,A)\\,P_\\theta(dx)=P_\\theta(A)\\qquad(A\\in\\mathcal A).$$\nLet $\\{*\\}$ carry its full sigma-algebra, let $G_{0,\\theta}(*,A):=Q_0(A)$,\nand recursively compose the probability kernel $G_{j,\\theta}$ from $\\{*\\}$ to\n$\\mathcal X$ with $K_\\theta$ to obtain $G_{j+1,\\theta}$. Define\n$Q_{j,\\theta}(A):=G_{j,\\theta}(*,A)$; thus $Q_{0,\\theta}=Q_0$ and\n$$Q_{j+1,\\theta}(A)=\\int_\\mathcal X K_\\theta(x,A)\\,Q_{j,\\theta}(dx),\\qquad A\\in\\mathcal A.$$\nFinite probability-kernel composition makes each $Q_{j,\\theta}$ a probability\nmeasure. For an integer $k\\ge1$, $Q_{k,\\theta}$ is the $k$-step reconstruction law.\n\nIf $Q_0$ and $Q_{k,\\theta}$ have specified nonnegative measurable densities\n$r_{0,\\theta}$ and $r_{k,\\theta}$ relative to $P_\\theta$, meaning\n$Q_{j,\\theta}(A)=\\int_A r_{j,\\theta}\\,dP_\\theta$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$\nfor $j\\in\\{0,k\\}$, and\n$$\\int|r_{j,\\theta}\\log r_{j,\\theta}|\\,dP_\\theta<\\infty\\qquad(j\\in\\{0,k\\}),$$\ndefine their finite contrastive-divergence objective by\n$$\\operatorname{CD}_k(\\theta):=\\int r_{0,\\theta}\\log r_{0,\\theta}\\,dP_\\theta-\\int r_{k,\\theta}\\log r_{k,\\theta}\\,dP_\\theta,$$\nwhere $0\\log0:=0$ and $\\log$ is the natural logarithm. On a finite state\nspace where $P_\\theta$ has positive mass at every state, write\n$p_\\theta(x):=P_\\theta(\\{x\\})$ and $q_{j,\\theta}(x):=Q_{j,\\theta}(\\{x\\})$.\nThen $r_{j,\\theta}(x)=q_{j,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x)$ and the objective equals\n$$\\sum_{x:q_{0,\\theta}(x)>0}q_{0,\\theta}(x)\\log\\frac{q_{0,\\theta}(x)}{p_\\theta(x)}-\\sum_{x:q_{k,\\theta}(x)>0}q_{k,\\theta}(x)\\log\\frac{q_{k,\\theta}(x)}{p_\\theta(x)},$$\nsince each finite-space integral is the sum of the integrand times the point\nmass and $p_\\theta r_{j,\\theta}=q_{j,\\theta}$.\n\nFor a positive integer $m$, the procedure takes independent data draws\n$x^1,\\ldots,x^m$ with common law $Q_0$, a transition sampler for $K_\\theta$,\nand a learning rate $\\eta>0$; the\nreconstruction chains are drawn independently conditional on their starts. Its\noutput is the updated parameter from the **stop-gradient CD-$k$ update**:\n\n1. For each $i$, set $x^{i,0}=x^i$ and draw $x^{i,j+1}$ from\n   $K_\\theta(x^{i,j},\\cdot)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,k-1$.\n2. Form\n   $$\\widehat g_k=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\left(\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta(x^i)-\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta(x^{i,k})\\right).$$\n3. Update $\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta\\widehat g_k$.\n\nWhen $\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta$ is integrable under $Q_0$ and $Q_{k,\\theta}$,\nthe associated **population CD-$k$ direction** is\n$$g_k(\\theta):=\\int\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta\\,dQ_0-\\int\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta\\,dQ_{k,\\theta}.$$\nThe procedure makes no claim that the chain has mixed in $k$ steps. The\nparameter derivative of $Q_{k,\\theta}$ is not stopped in the scalar objective's\ndefinition; the update above deliberately omits that derivative.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-contrastive-divergence-k",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $r$ be a positive\ninteger and $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^r$,\nand let $E_\\theta:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable in $x$ and continuously\ndifferentiable in $\\theta$. Define the energy model $P_\\theta$ relative to $\\mu$\nonly when\n$$0<Z_\\theta:=\\int_\\mathcal X e^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\,d\\mu(x)<\\infty,$$\nwith density $e^{-E_\\theta}/Z_\\theta$. Let $Q_0$ be a data probability\nmeasure on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ and let $K_\\theta$ be a probability kernel\nfrom $\\mathcal X$ to itself which leaves $P_\\theta$ invariant, meaning\n$$\\int_\\mathcal X K_\\theta(x,A)\\,P_\\theta(dx)=P_\\theta(A)\\qquad(A\\in\\mathcal A).$$\nLet $\\{*\\}$ carry its full sigma-algebra, let $G_{0,\\theta}(*,A):=Q_0(A)$,\nand recursively compose the probability kernel $G_{j,\\theta}$ from $\\{*\\}$ to\n$\\mathcal X$ with $K_\\theta$ to obtain $G_{j+1,\\theta}$. Define\n$Q_{j,\\theta}(A):=G_{j,\\theta}(*,A)$; thus $Q_{0,\\theta}=Q_0$ and\n$$Q_{j+1,\\theta}(A)=\\int_\\mathcal X K_\\theta(x,A)\\,Q_{j,\\theta}(dx),\\qquad A\\in\\mathcal A.$$\nFinite probability-kernel composition makes each $Q_{j,\\theta}$ a probability\nmeasure. For an integer $k\\ge1$, $Q_{k,\\theta}$ is the $k$-step reconstruction law.\n\nIf $Q_0$ and $Q_{k,\\theta}$ have specified nonnegative measurable densities\n$r_{0,\\theta}$ and $r_{k,\\theta}$ relative to $P_\\theta$, meaning\n$Q_{j,\\theta}(A)=\\int_A r_{j,\\theta}\\,dP_\\theta$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$\nfor $j\\in\\{0,k\\}$, and\n$$\\int|r_{j,\\theta}\\log r_{j,\\theta}|\\,dP_\\theta<\\infty\\qquad(j\\in\\{0,k\\}),$$\ndefine their finite contrastive-divergence objective by\n$$\\operatorname{CD}_k(\\theta):=\\int r_{0,\\theta}\\log r_{0,\\theta}\\,dP_\\theta-\\int r_{k,\\theta}\\log r_{k,\\theta}\\,dP_\\theta,$$\nwhere $0\\log0:=0$ and $\\log$ is the natural logarithm. On a finite state\nspace where $P_\\theta$ has positive mass at every state, write\n$p_\\theta(x):=P_\\theta(\\{x\\})$ and $q_{j,\\theta}(x):=Q_{j,\\theta}(\\{x\\})$.\nThen $r_{j,\\theta}(x)=q_{j,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x)$ and the objective equals\n$$\\sum_{x:q_{0,\\theta}(x)>0}q_{0,\\theta}(x)\\log\\frac{q_{0,\\theta}(x)}{p_\\theta(x)}-\\sum_{x:q_{k,\\theta}(x)>0}q_{k,\\theta}(x)\\log\\frac{q_{k,\\theta}(x)}{p_\\theta(x)},$$\nsince each finite-space integral is the sum of the integrand times the point\nmass and $p_\\theta r_{j,\\theta}=q_{j,\\theta}$.\n\nFor a positive integer $m$, the procedure takes independent data draws\n$x^1,\\ldots,x^m$ with common law $Q_0$, a transition sampler for $K_\\theta$,\nand a learning rate $\\eta>0$; the\nreconstruction chains are drawn independently conditional on their starts. Its\noutput is the updated parameter from the **stop-gradient CD-$k$ update**:\n\n1. For each $i$, set $x^{i,0}=x^i$ and draw $x^{i,j+1}$ from\n   $K_\\theta(x^{i,j},\\cdot)$ for $j=0,\\ldots,k-1$.\n2. Form\n   $$\\widehat g_k=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\left(\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta(x^i)-\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta(x^{i,k})\\right).$$\n3. Update $\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta\\widehat g_k$.\n\nWhen $\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta$ is integrable under $Q_0$ and $Q_{k,\\theta}$,\nthe associated **population CD-$k$ direction** is\n$$g_k(\\theta):=\\int\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta\\,dQ_0-\\int\\nabla_\\theta E_\\theta\\,dQ_{k,\\theta}.$$\nThe procedure makes no claim that the chain has mixed in $k$ steps. The\nparameter derivative of $Q_{k,\\theta}$ is not stopped in the scalar objective's\ndefinition; the update above deliberately omits that derivative.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty set. A **probability vector** on\n$\\mathcal X$ is a family $P=(p_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$ of real numbers such that\n$p_x\\ge 0$ for every $x$ and $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}p_x=1$. Its support is\n$\\operatorname{supp}P:=\\{x\\in\\mathcal X:p_x>0\\}$. Coordinates outside the\nsupport are retained as zeros; they are not deleted from the alphabet.\n\nFor another probability vector $Q=(q_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$, the notation\n$P\\ll Q$ means $\\operatorname{supp}P\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}Q$. All\nfinite sums over $\\mathcal X$ use the displayed alphabet and ordinary finite\nreal arithmetic, extended to $+\\infty$ only where a later definition says so.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-derivative",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), neighbourhoods are those of\n[[def-neighbourhood-r]] and limit points those of [[def-limit-point-r]].\n\nLet $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $c \\in A$ be a\n**limit point of $A$**. The **difference quotient of $f$ at $c$** is the function\n\n$$q_{f,c} : A \\setminus \\{c\\} \\to \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad q_{f,c}(x) := \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c} .$$\n\nThe division is legitimate at every point of the domain, since $x \\ne c$ gives\n$x - c \\ne 0$.\n\n**The point $c$ is a limit point of $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$, not merely of $A$.**\nFor every real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the punctured neighbourhood\n$N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(c)$ omits $c$, so\n\n$$N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(c) \\cap A \\;=\\; N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(c) \\cap (A \\setminus \\{c\\}) ,$$\n\nand the left-hand side is nonempty because $c$ is a limit point of $A$. So\n$q_{f,c}$ is a function on a set having $c$ as a limit point, and\n$\\lim_{x \\to c} q_{f,c}(x)$ is a notion that [[def-function-limit]] defines.\n\n$f$ is **differentiable at $c$** when that limit exists, and then the\n**derivative of $f$ at $c$** is\n\n$$f'(c) \\;:=\\; \\lim_{x \\to c} q_{f,c}(x) \\;=\\; \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c} .$$\n\n**Two obligations are carried by that notation, and both are discharged here.**\n\n1. **Uniqueness.** Writing $f'(c)$ treats the right-hand side as a name for a\n   single real number. That is legitimate: $c$ is a limit point of the domain\n   $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$ of $q_{f,c}$, so at most one real can satisfy the\n   $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, by [[lem-function-limit-unique]] applied to\n   $q_{f,c}$. Two reals both meeting the condition are therefore equal, and the\n   symbol denotes.\n2. **Meaningfulness.** The hypothesis that $c$ is a limit point of $A$ is not\n   decoration. At an **isolated** point of $A$ the punctured condition\n   $0 < |x - c| < \\delta$ is met by no point of the domain at all, so the\n   $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ formula is satisfied vacuously by *every* real at\n   once; this is why [[def-function-limit]] leaves the limit undefined there,\n   and it is why this library defines $f'(c)$ only at a limit point of $A$. At\n   an isolated point of its domain a function is neither differentiable nor\n   non-differentiable here: the question is not posed.\n\n**The limit sees only $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$, so how the difference quotient is\nextended to $c$ is irrelevant.** Let $Q : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ agree with\n$q_{f,c}$ at every point of $A \\setminus \\{c\\}$, and let $L \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nThen $\\lim_{x \\to c} Q(x) = L$ if and only if $\\lim_{x \\to c} q_{f,c}(x) = L$.\nBoth conditions read: for every real $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real\n$\\delta > 0$ such that every point $x$ of the relevant domain with\n$0 < |x - c| < \\delta$ satisfies $|{\\cdot} - L| < \\varepsilon$\n([[def-function-limit]]). The clause $0 < |x - c|$ removes $x = c$ from both\nquantifiers, so in both cases the points quantified over are exactly the\n$x \\in A \\setminus \\{c\\}$ with $0 < |x - c| < \\delta$, at which $Q$ and\n$q_{f,c}$ take the same value. The two conditions are the same condition.\n\n**Differentiability on a set.** For $S \\subseteq A$, $f$ is **differentiable on\n$S$** when it is differentiable at every $c \\in S$; implicit in that phrase is\nthat every point of $S$ is a limit point of $A$. $f$ is **differentiable** when\nit is differentiable on the whole of $A$.\n\n**Restriction of the domain.** Let $B \\subseteq A$, let $c \\in B$ and suppose\n$c$ is a limit point of $B$. If $f$ is differentiable at $c$, then so is the\nrestriction $f|_B : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and\n\n$$(f|_B)'(c) \\;=\\; f'(c) .$$\n\nIndeed $B \\setminus \\{c\\} \\subseteq A \\setminus \\{c\\}$; the displayed identity\nof punctured neighbourhoods above, applied to $B$, shows that $c$ is a limit\npoint of $B \\setminus \\{c\\}$; the difference quotient $q_{f|_B, c}$ is the\nrestriction of $q_{f,c}$ to $B \\setminus \\{c\\}$, since $f|_B(c) = f(c)$; and\nclaim 2 of [[lem-limit-is-local]] carries the limit to that restriction.\n\n**Every point of a nondegenerate interval is a limit point of it.** Let\n$J \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be order-convex ([[def-interval]]) with at least two\nelements and let $p \\in J$. Choose $q \\in J$ with $q \\ne p$, and let a real\n$\\varepsilon > 0$ be given. If $p < q$, put $y := p + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\min\\{\\varepsilon,\\ q - p\\}$;\nthen $p < y$, and $y - p \\le \\tfrac{1}{2}(q-p) < q - p$, so $p < y < q$ and\norder-convexity gives $y \\in J$, while $0 < |y - p| < \\varepsilon$. If $q < p$,\nthe point $y := p - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\min\\{\\varepsilon,\\ p - q\\}$ serves in the same\nway. So $N^{*}_{\\varepsilon}(p) \\cap J \\ne \\varnothing$ for every real\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, that is, $p$ is a limit point of $J$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]).\n\nConsequently, for $f$ defined on a nondegenerate interval $I$, the symbol\n$f'(c)$ is meaningful at **every** $c \\in I$, endpoints included. At an endpoint\nthe difference quotient is taken over the points of $I$ lying on the one side\nthat is available, so what other texts call a one-sided derivative is, here,\nsimply the derivative of $f$ on $I$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $\\mathcal A$ be a sigma-algebra on $X$ ([[def-sigma-algebra]]). A **measure** on $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a function\n\n$$\\mu:\\mathcal A\\longrightarrow[0,+\\infty]\\subseteq\\overline{\\mathbb R}$$\n\nsuch that:\n\n1. $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$;\n2. for every pairwise disjoint sequence $(E_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb N}$ in $\\mathcal A$,\n   $$\\mu\\left(\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb N}E_k\\right)=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\mu(E_k),$$\n   where the right side is the nonnegative extended sum of [[def-nonnegative-extended-series]].\n\nThe second condition is **countable additivity**. It begins at index $0$ and includes the case in which some term or the total sum is $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-probability-measure",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **probability measure** on a measurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$ is a measure $\\mathbb P$ with $\\mathbb P(X)=1$ ([[def-measure]]). The triple $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$ is a **probability space** ([[def-measure-space]]); $X$ is its sample space and the members of $\\mathcal A$ are its events.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra, and $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable. A **conditional-expectation version of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$** is a real, $\\mathcal G$-measurable, integrable function $Y$ such that $\\int_A Y\\,dP=\\int_A X\\,dP$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$. No completeness of $\\mathcal G$ is assumed.\n\nExpectation means the integral of [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. Integrable representatives use [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]; quotient notation is introduced after uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\exp(x)>0$ and\n$$\\exp(-x)=\\frac1{\\exp(x)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "exact-likelihood-gradient",
          "claim": "Fix a coordinate $j$ and vary only $\\theta_j$ in a small interval inside the open set $\\Theta$. Since $\\mathcal X$ is finite and each energy is real, [F13] makes every $e^{-E_\\theta(x)}$ positive; hence [F1] gives $0<Z_\\theta<\\infty$. The formula for $p_\\theta$ then gives positive coordinates summing to one, so it is a probability vector by [F4]. By [F6], [F7] and [F8], differentiating the finite sum gives $\\partial_jZ_\\theta=-\\sum_xe^{-E_\\theta(x)}\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)$. The same chain and quotient rules give $\\partial_jp_\\theta(x)/p_\\theta(x)=-\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)-\\partial_jZ_\\theta/Z_\\theta=-\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)+\\sum_yp_\\theta(y)\\partial_jE_\\theta(y)$; [F9] identifies the left side with $\\partial_j\\log p_\\theta(x)$. Because $q_0$ is fixed and sums to one by [F4], differentiating $\\mathcal L=-\\sum_xq_0(x)\\log p_\\theta(x)$ and summing this identity yields the displayed likelihood-gradient formula.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F13",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "differentiate-reconstruction-law",
          "claim": "The recurrence in [F2] gives $q_{j+1,\\theta}=q_{j,\\theta}K_\\theta$, hence $q_{k,\\theta}=q_0K_\\theta^k$ and $\\sum_xq_{k,\\theta}(x)=1$. For $k=1$ the product rule [F6] gives $\\partial_j(q_0K_\\theta)=q_0\\partial_jK_\\theta$. If $\\partial_j(q_0K_\\theta^k)=\\sum_{s=0}^{k-1}q_0K_\\theta^s(\\partial_jK_\\theta)K_\\theta^{k-1-s}$, then entrywise application of [F6] to $q_0K_\\theta^kK_\\theta$ yields $\\sum_{s=0}^{k-1}q_0K_\\theta^s(\\partial_jK_\\theta)K_\\theta^{k-s}+q_0K_\\theta^k(\\partial_jK_\\theta)$, which is the same formula with $k+1$ by appending the $s=k$ term. Thus the stated product derivative holds for every $k$. Differentiating $\\sum_xq_{k,\\theta}(x)=1$ by [F6] gives $\\sum_x\\partial_jq_{k,\\theta}(x)=0$. Strict positivity of $K_\\theta$ and nonzero mass of $q_0$ imply $q_{k,\\theta}(x)>0$ for every $x$, so the following logarithms are finite.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Put $w(x_0,\\ldots,x_k):=q_0(x_0)\\prod_{s=0}^{k-1}K_\\theta(x_s,x_{s+1})$. These weights are nonnegative. Summing successively over $x_k,\\ldots,x_1$ and using the row sums of $K_\\theta$ reduces $\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)$ to $\\sum_{x_0}q_0(x_0)=1$. The set function $\\mathbb P_\\theta(A)=\\sum_{\\omega\\in A}w(\\omega)$ vanishes on the empty set and is countably additive: a disjoint sequence of subsets of finite $\\Omega$ has only finitely many nonempty terms. Thus [F10] makes it a measure, and [F11] makes it a probability measure.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "differentiate-reconstruction-kl",
          "claim": "By the quotient rule [F6], $\\partial_j(q_{k,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x))=\\partial_jq_{k,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x)-q_{k,\\theta}(x)\\partial_jp_\\theta(x)/p_\\theta(x)^2$; the chain and logarithm derivative rules [F7, F9] then give $\\partial_j\\log(q_{k,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x))=\\partial_jq_{k,\\theta}(x)/q_{k,\\theta}(x)-\\partial_j\\log p_\\theta(x)$. Applying the product rule [F6] to each summand of $D_{\\rm KL}(q_{k,\\theta}\\|p_\\theta)$ therefore gives $\\partial_jq_{k,\\theta}(x)(\\log(q_{k,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x))+1)-q_{k,\\theta}(x)\\partial_j\\log p_\\theta(x)$. Summing the finite terms, the $+1$ contribution vanishes by step 1.2. Substitution from step 1.1 yields $\\partial_jD_{\\rm KL}(q_{k,\\theta}\\|p_\\theta)=\\sum_x\\partial_jq_{k,\\theta}(x)\\log(q_{k,\\theta}(x)/p_\\theta(x))+\\sum_xq_{k,\\theta}(x)\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)-\\sum_xp_\\theta(x)\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "claim": "For $s=0$, $K_\\theta^0=I$ is row-stochastic and its entries are the length-zero path weights. If the entries of $K_\\theta^s$ sum the products along all length-$s$ paths, then $K_\\theta^{s+1}(x,y)=\\sum_zK_\\theta^s(x,z)K_\\theta(z,y)$ appends the final transition, so its entries sum the length-$(s+1)$ path weights; also $\\sum_yK_\\theta^{s+1}(x,y)=\\sum_zK_\\theta^s(x,z)\\sum_yK_\\theta(z,y)=1$. Thus $\\mathbb P_\\theta(X_0=x,X_k=y)=q_0(x)K_\\theta^k(x,y)$. The function $g_f$ is finite-valued; for each Borel $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, $\\{g_f(X_0)\\in C\\}=X_0^{-1}(\\{x:g_f(x)\\in C\\})$, so $g_f(X_0)$ is $\\sigma(X_0)$-measurable. Both $f(X_k)$ and $g_f(X_0)$ are integrable since $\\Omega$ is finite.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "subtract-kl-derivatives",
          "claim": "Since $q_0$ is fixed, the terms $q_0(x)\\log q_0(x)$ in $D_{\\rm KL}(q_0\\|p_\\theta)$ have zero derivative; applying the same product and logarithm rules [F6, F9] and using the log-density formula from step 1.1 and $\\sum_xq_0(x)=1$ gives $\\partial_jD_{\\rm KL}(q_0\\|p_\\theta)=\\sum_xq_0(x)\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)-\\sum_xp_\\theta(x)\\partial_jE_\\theta(x)$. Subtracting step 2.1 cancels the model expectations and yields the stated $\\partial_j\\operatorname{CD}_k$ formula. By [F3], the algorithm uses only its first two terms; the remaining sum is the derivative through $K_\\theta^k$. Comparing with step 1.1 also shows that the algorithm replaces the exact likelihood negative phase $p_\\theta$ by $q_{k,\\theta}$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Every event in $\\sigma(X_0)$ has the form $\\{X_0\\in B\\}$ for some $B\\subseteq\\mathcal X$, since these preimages form a sigma-algebra and generate $\\sigma(X_0)$. For every such event, step 2.2 gives $$\\int \\mathbf1_{\\{X_0\\in B\\}}f(X_k)\\,d\\mathbb P_\\theta=\\sum_{x\\in B}q_0(x)\\sum_yK_\\theta^k(x,y)f(y)=\\int \\mathbf1_{\\{X_0\\in B\\}}g_f(X_0)\\,d\\mathbb P_\\theta,$$ where terms with $q_0(x)=0$ vanish. The integrability and measurability from step 2.2 and this event-integral identity satisfy [F12], so $g_f(X_0)$ is the asserted conditional-expectation version.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F12",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [],
